Z||000000||Svyn||10-09-2001||10:44 PM||||Found this on the Sccoa Fourm..<P>A Father and his son are walking the Manhattan streets when the father stops at a vacant lot, takes a deep breath and tells his son: "To think that one time here on this very lot stood the Twin Towers". The son looks at his dad and asks "Dad, what are the Twin Towers?" The father says: "My dear son, the Twin Towers were two tremendously tall building's with lot's of offices that was the heart of the United-States, but approx. 31 years ago, several Arabs destroyed the Building's". The boy thinks for a minute and then ask's his Father. "Daddy what are Arabs?"<P>Makes you think, does it not...<P>------------------<BR>-Dale Clark<BR>86' Fiero SE<BR>99' Dodge Durango SLT+<BR>92' Ford ThunderBird SuperCoupe||||reg||3
Z||000001||beer_stud_21||10-09-2001||10:59 PM||||I sure hope that doesn't happen.  That's blowing things WAY out of proportion.  I think the US is going about this the right way so far.  There have already been reports of civilian casualties from the two days of bombing, but that has got to be expected to a certain extent.  Just so long as we make damn sure we let these people know  who we consider friend and who we consider fo I dont think we have to worry about getting into a holy war.  Lets just not bomb any place that has religious significance or something.  I read that  16% of the worlds population is Muslim...  That is a lot of potential Bin Laden recruits.  Lets not give them any reasons...<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||10-10-2001||12:41 AM||||I've seen various versions of this around lately. Indiscriminate killing even in war is not the way to win friends in a place we already have few enough. I wonder tho, what would be the correct reaction be if a nuclear device was set off, say in N.Y., L.A., or  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> , San Angelo Tex? Any 'doves'<BR>got a comment? Stir, stir, stir.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||10-10-2001||12:56 AM||||MJ: I'm BAAaaaaaack  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Still putting my notes together for our little off-forum discussion...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But anyhoo: <P>As of 1996, there were 334 MILLION living in the middle east. I imagine that number has grown somewhat. The fact is that there isn't a whole lot of support for the Taliban. The arab nations have been on their way to peace before any of this started. Several years ago, most of them would have been banding up and fighting the US. But now, most of the nations are anti-terrorist, and have anti-terrorist policies in place. Saudi and Egypt for starters. The Taliban will hopefully be wiped off the planet. I feel for the poor illiterate bastards that have been raised and brainwashed to hating a nation they have no experience of, but it's them or us. I can pretty much guarantee that if they lay down the arms in front of US Soldiers that they would be taken as POWs and treated fairly. They have that option. <P>The Afghan populace has been paralyzed for years, first by civil war, and then massive oppression by the Taliban. Most of them would rather that the US would go home, and that the Taliban had never existed. I hope for a peaceful end to it, but there's going to be a lot of blood to wade through, first. Justice is Justice. All the Taliban had to do was turn that animal over. Even if he DIDN'T do the WTC, he's responsible for a hell of a lot more...<P>There will be civilian casualties. I wish there wouldn't. But I blame the cowards that make up the Taliban. They've always used the innocents as human sheilds. Keep up the discretion USA, Try and make the only thing to hit the civilians are Taliban body parts. ||||reg||14
Z||000004||My7Fieros||10-10-2001||01:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Svyn:<BR><B>Found this on the Sccoa Fourm..<P>A Father and his son are walking the Manhattan streets when the father stops at a vacant lot, takes a deep breath and tells his son: "To think that one time here on this very lot stood the Twin Towers". The son looks at his dad and asks "Dad, what are the Twin Towers?" The father says: "My dear son, the Twin Towers were two tremendously tall building's with lot's of offices that was the heart of the United-States, but approx. 31 years ago, several Arabs destroyed the Building's". The boy thinks for a minute and then ask's his Father. "Daddy what are Arabs?"<P>Makes you think, does it not...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Actually it doesn't...except for the twin towers part.........................<P><BR><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="blue"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fogglethorpe||10-10-2001||08:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>I've seen various versions of this around lately. Indiscriminate killing even in war is not the way to win friends in a place we already have few enough. I wonder tho, what would be the correct reaction be if a nuclear device was set off, say in N.Y., L.A., or   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> , San Angelo Tex? Any 'doves'<BR>got a comment? Stir, stir, stir.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Win friends? That's not what war is about. The sole purpose of war is to kill people and break things, until the enemy is sufficiently weakened into surrender.<BR>But, I absolutely do not favor harming innocent women and children intentionally.<BR>One more thing...<BR>In WW2, Pres. Truman had a heart-wrenching decision to make; either risk the deaths of two million more Americans on the ground in Japan, or drop the big one on a city full of innocent civilians. We all know what he chose...and three days later, when they still did not surrender, he chose to drop one on another city, wiping out thousands more civilians. At that point, Japan was eager to surrender unconditionally.<BR>It may sound cruel, but many more lives were saved because of his decision...AMERICAN lives.<BR>That's the cruel reality of war. There are no rules but winning.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||10-11-2001||12:27 AM||||I was refering to the nations we need to launch attacks from, such as Pakistan. And you're right about winning a war. Things were different in Truman's time. No such thing as political correctness then. Too many whiney butts now days. A lot of people still beleive in 'peace at any cost'. Kinda like the 'better red than dead' thing in the cold war. The problem stems from most people that were in a 'hot' war are now pretty old, and not many people give them much credence, other than patronization. We stopped electing officials that would keep us free a long time ago. Electing people to keep our economy strong and govt subsidies going became more important. I think I may have mentioned it here once or twice before-The"It doesn't concern me, it's way over there" syndrome. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
